WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.999
Identity and access review is central to
cloud security assessment because cloud

2
00:00:07.079 --> 00:00:10.282
control is heavily permission-driven.

3
00:00:10.362 --> 00:00:15.448
The review should consider user
identities, service accounts,

4
00:00:15.528 --> 00:00:20.991
workload identities, roles, policies,
groups, privileged access,

5
00:00:21.071 --> 00:00:27.571
inactive accounts, exposed credentials,
cross-account or cross-tenant trust,

6
00:00:27.651 --> 00:00:30.194
and emergency access patterns.

7
00:00:30.274 --> 00:00:35.454
The question is not only who exists, but
what each identity can do.

8
00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:40.338
AI can summarize permission patterns
across large exports.

9
00:00:40.418 --> 00:00:45.410
It can group identities by role, highlight
broad permissions,

10
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:50.482
list inactive accounts for review, compare
similar policies,

11
00:00:50.562 --> 00:00:53.106
and draft questions for owners.

12
00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:59.496
It can also identify places where a
service identity appears to have more access

13
00:00:59.576 --> 00:01:02.402
than its stated function requires.

14
00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:07.003
These outputs are starting points, not
final judgments.

15
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:09.909
Least privilege requires context.

16
00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:15.735
A permission that looks broad may be
necessary for a deployment service,

17
00:01:15.815 --> 00:01:21.749
while a smaller permission may be risky if
attached to an exposed workload.

18
00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:27.292
Reviewers need to understand business
purpose, workload behavior,

19
00:01:27.372 --> 00:01:33.495
automation needs, and compensating
controls before recommending changes.

20
00:01:33.575 --> 00:01:38.661
AI should not overclaim risk from
permissions without context.

21
00:01:38.741 --> 00:01:43.545
Credential exposure and stale access
deserve special care.

22
00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:48.805
Long-lived keys, inactive users, unused
roles, weak ownership,

23
00:01:48.885 --> 00:01:52.936
or unclear trust relationships can
increase risk.

24
00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:58.667
Assessment findings should describe the
evidence, the likely impact,

25
00:01:58.747 --> 00:02:00.914
and the validation needed.

26
00:02:00.994 --> 00:02:04.667
If a credential or secret appears in
evidence,

27
00:02:04.747 --> 00:02:09.739
handling should follow approved
sensitive-data procedures.

28
00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:13.775
Human review is required before changing
access.

29
00:02:13.855 --> 00:02:16.398
Removing a role, rotating a key,

30
00:02:16.478 --> 00:02:21.282
or disabling an identity can interrupt
production systems.

31
00:02:21.362 --> 00:02:27.673
The assessment should produce clear
remediation options, owner assignments,

32
00:02:27.753 --> 00:02:29.543
and validation steps.

33
00:02:29.623 --> 00:02:33.767
The safest outcome is not instant
automated change;

34
00:02:33.847 --> 00:02:38.180
it is controlled improvement toward least
privilege.
